1. Multi-Tenancy Patterns
  - Kubernetes multi-tenancy is the practice of sharing a Kubernetes cluster among multiple tenants while maintaining isolation, security, and resource fairness. A tenant could be a team, department, customer, application, or environment.
  - Common Multi-Tenancy Models
  1. Namespace-Based Multi-Tenancy (Soft Multi-Tenancy)
    The most common approach.
      Cluster
      ├── namespace: team-a
      ├── namespace: team-b
      └── namespace: team-c
    Each tenant gets its own namespace.
    Isolation mechanisms:
      Namespaces
      RBAC
      Network Policies
      Resource Quotas
      Limit Ranges
    Example:
      apiVersion: v1
      kind: ResourceQuota
      metadata:
        name: team-a-quota
        namespace: team-a
      spec:
        hard:
          requests.cpu: "10"
          requests.memory: 20Gi
    Pros
      Low cost
      Easy management
      Efficient resource utilization
    Cons
      Shared control plane
      Weaker isolation
      Cluster-wide misconfigurations can affect all tenants
    Best for
      Internal engineering teams
      Development environments
      Small to medium organizations
  
  2. Cluster-Based Multi-Tenancy (Hard Multi-Tenancy)
    Each tenant gets a dedicated Kubernetes cluster.
      Tenant A → Cluster A
      Tenant B → Cluster B
      Tenant C → Cluster C
    Pros
      Strong isolation
      Independent upgrades
      Separate security boundaries
    Cons
      Higher operational cost
      More infrastructure overhead
      Cluster sprawl
    Best for
      SaaS platforms with strict customer isolation
      Regulated industries
      Enterprise customers
  
  3. Virtual Cluster Pattern
    - A virtual cluster runs inside a namespace of a host cluster while exposing a separate Kubernetes API server to tenants.
      Host Cluster
      ├── vCluster A
      ├── vCluster B
      └── vCluster C
    - Popular solutions:
      vCluster
      Loft Labs
    - How it works
      Tenant sees their own cluster.
      Workloads run in namespaces on the host cluster.
      Separate API server and control plane.
    - Pros
      Better isolation than namespaces
      Lower cost than dedicated clusters
      Self-service environments
    - Cons
      Additional operational complexity
      Some Kubernetes features require special handling
    - Best for
      Platform engineering teams
      CI/CD preview environments
      Internal developer platforms
  
  4. Hierarchical Namespace Pattern
    - Useful for large organizations.
      company
      ├── platform
      │   ├── backend
      │   └── frontend
      └── data
          ├── analytics
          └── ml
    - Implemented using:
      Hierarchical Namespace Controller (HNC)
    - Benefits
      Delegated administration
      Policy inheritance
      Better organizational structure

2. Isolation Layers in Multi-Tenant Clusters
  - Identity Isolation
  Use RBAC to restrict access.
    Tenant → Role → RoleBinding → Namespace
  - Example:
    kind: RoleBinding
    apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
    metadata:
      namespace: team-a
      name: team-a-admin
    subjects:
    - kind: User
      name: alice
    roleRef:
      kind: Role
      name: admin
      apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

  - Network Isolation
  Use Network Policies.
    team-a pods
        ↓
    only communicate
        ↓
    team-a services
  - Example:
    apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
    kind: NetworkPolicy
    metadata:
      namespace: team-a
      name: deny-cross-namespace
    spec:
      podSelector: {}
      policyTypes:
      - Ingress
  - Popular implementations:
    Cilium
    Calico
  
  - Resource Isolation
  Prevent "noisy neighbor" issues.
  Tools:
    ResourceQuota
    LimitRange
    CPU/Memory requests and limits
      Tenant A
        CPU: 10 cores

      Tenant B
        CPU: 10 cores

      Tenant C
        CPU: 10 cores
  No tenant can starve others.
  
  - Security Isolation
  Use:
    Pod Security Admission
    OPA/Gatekeeper
    Kyverno
  - Examples:
    Disallow privileged containers
    Enforce image registries
    Require resource limits
  - Popular projects:
    OPA Gatekeeper
    Kyverno

3. Multi-Tenancy Patterns Used by Platform Teams
  Pattern 1: Namespace per Team
    platform-cluster
    ├── team-a
    ├── team-b
    ├── team-c
  Most common enterprise model.

  Pattern 2: Namespace per Environment
    team-a-dev
    team-a-stage
    team-a-prod
  Simple but can create many namespaces.

  Pattern 3: Cluster per Environment
    dev-cluster
    staging-cluster
    prod-cluster
  Frequently used in larger organizations.

  Pattern 4: Virtual Cluster per Team
    shared-cluster
    ├── vcluster-team-a
    ├── vcluster-team-b
    └── vcluster-team-c
  Increasingly popular for Internal Developer Platforms (IDPs).

4. Recommended Pattern by Organization Size
  Organization	                Recommended Pattern
  Startup	                      Namespace-based
  Growing company	              Namespace + Network Policies + Quotas
  Large enterprise	            Virtual clusters or hierarchical namespaces
  Multi-customer SaaS	          Dedicated clusters or virtual clusters
  Highly regulated workloads	  Dedicated clusters